prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 6–10 sentences: I want to start a new feature called `task-snooze` in a kdevkit-aware project. What do you ask in the Requirements interview, and how do you write the spec when I answer? Cover the framing of the Requirements prompt, how Requirements pairs with the project's tests, and where library / file-path / function names belong.
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers four behaviors. (1) **Three-layer framing of the spec.** The agent names that the spec separates capability (Feature Brief — what the user can now do) from experience (Requirements — what the user touches and observes) from design (Design — how it's built: schemas, plumbing, libraries). Either the literal three-word triple "capability / experience / design" or any phrasing that names all three layers and assigns each to its section is sufficient. (2) **Requirements interview framing — experience layer.** The agent's Requirements prompt asks the user how they *experience* the capability — what they touch, what they observe. Acceptable concrete framings include CLI (flags and output), app (screens and visible state), skill change (cues the agent recognises and artefacts it produces), or service (request shape and response); naming at least one feature-type example shows the agent isn't defaulting to CLI-thinking. The prompt explicitly excludes library names, internal file paths, function/schema names, and protocol verbs — those go in Design. (3) **Smell test before each Requirements bullet.** The agent says it sanity-checks each Requirements bullet before writing it: a library/framework name, an internal file path or config key the user doesn't see, a function/class/trait/schema name, or an internal protocol verb / hook event name. Bullets that name any of those move to Design. The literal phrase "smell test" is not required — "before each bullet I check whether it names any of these and move it to Design if so" or equivalent is sufficient. (4) **V-model pairing of Requirements and Design with the project's test layers.** The agent describes that Requirements pairs with functional / integration tests (which verify in user-observable terms) and Design pairs with unit tests (which verify design primitives). Either the literal "V-model" reference or any phrasing that names the pairing — "functional tests verify Requirements; unit tests verify Design" or equivalent — is sufficient. The agent SHOULD frame this as guidance / default rather than rigid form: the spec layout adapts to the feature, and the strictness lives in the §6 / §7 / §8 gates. Wrong answers: producing a spec where Requirements names libraries / file paths / function names; treating "what the user can do" (capability) and "what the user sees" (experience) as the same layer; defaulting to CLI examples without acknowledging app / skill-change / service shapes; describing the spec layout as a strict heading order rather than guidance with V-model pairing as the default; placing functional tests after Design where they end up phrased against internal record-shape assertions; skipping the smell test entirely.
tools: claude,kiro
